  <!-- NAPERVILLE MASONRY PROFILE -->
  <section class="lp-section lp-profile" style="background:#E8E4DC;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Naperville Architecture &amp; Masonry Profile</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What Makes Naperville Masonry <em>Different</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-profile-grid">
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Primary Building Era</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">1840s – Present</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">1840s antique soft brick downtown through 1990s engineered veneers in the suburbs — no other DuPage County community needs this range of repair protocols.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Dominant Brick Type</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">Antique Soft Brick, Modular Face Brick &amp; Engineered Veneer</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">Antique soft brick and modern engineered veneer sit at opposite ends of the mortar hardness spectrum — treating them the same causes irreversible damage to both.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Climate Risk</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">High — 90+ Freeze-Thaw Cycles/Year</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">90+ freeze-thaw cycles annually push moisture deeper into every failing joint — accelerating spalling and water infiltration behind the brick face.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- COMMON MASONRY PROBLEMS -->
  <section class="lp-section lp-problems" style="background:#F5F3EF;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Common Masonry Problems in Naperville</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What We Fix — <em>Most Often in Naperville</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-problems-grid">
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Historic Downtown Soft Brick Joint Failure</h3>
          <p>Antique soft brick from the 1840s–1880s is destroyed by Portland cement mortar — severe spalling that damages irreplaceable material. Every historic district assessment starts with mortar hardness testing before any repair prescription.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Engineered Veneer Joint Separation on Newer Homes</h3>
          <p>Engineered veneer and structure move at different rates — joint separation lets water into the cavity wall and causes structural damage before any exterior signs appear. Early tuckpointing prevents costly remediation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Chimney Crown Failure on 1990s Construction</h3>
          <p>1990s chimney crowns are now 25–35 years old and cracking — letting water enter the structure and migrate down through the interior. Early crown repair prevents full chimney reconstruction.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Suburban Grade-Level Mortar Deterioration</h3>
          <p>Moderate-to-low-slope lots concentrate ground moisture at the foundation-to-brick interface. Grade-level joint failure on north and west elevations allows water into the foundation wall — often overlooked until damage is significant.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

